        "final_response": "I found no regression in the refactor, so I made no further edits.\n\nI re-read both changed methods against the original logic and the `User`, `Position` and repository classes. The behavior is the same in each case I checked:\n\n- **`apply` (user-service):** the BUY and SELL checks run in the same order with the same error messages. Each rejection still returns 400, and an unknown type still returns 400 with \"invalid type\". `u.getId()` is the same value as `uid`, because `findForUpdate(uid)` loaded that user. The helpers are called from inside the `@Transactional` method, so locking and the dirty-checked updates to the user and position still commit together. `positions.save` for a new position is unchanged.\n- **`trade` (trade-service):** the order of calls is unchanged: validate shares, parse the user id, check the user exists, check the symbol and price, apply to user-service, save the trade, publish. The rejection status codes and the swallowed publish error are the same.\n\nThe evidence you supplied doesn't show a failing request or status. It is only user-service Hibernate SQL for user creation, portfolio reads and username checks. None of those paths are affected by my changes. There is no `apply` call, no `select … for update`, and no 4xx or 5xx in it.\n\nGuessing at an edit would risk changing behavior without a cause. If you can send the failing acceptance scenario name, the expected and actual status code, or the trade-service and gateway logs from that run, I can target the fix. I can also revert the two refactors if you prefer to restore the baseline and leave the CCN findings unresolved.",
